  "body": "In #69 @tyoshino highlighted an underspecified/weird thing we're doing. Sentences like\n\n> Return a promise rejected with an error indicating that you cannot close a stream that is already closed.\n\nare strange in two ways:\n1. They don't specify what error type, exactly, we're using;\n2. Most (all?) specs don't include text describing what the error should say.\n\n---\n\nFor 1, the issue here is that, if we're trying to stay on the level of JS, we don't have access to the `DOMException` hierarchy, so we can't use a `DOMException` with `name = \"InvalidStateError\"` or similar. We're instead restricted to `EvalError`, `RangeError`, `ReferenceError`, `SyntaxError`, `TypeError`, and `URIError`---none of which seem to fit. I will email es-discuss to see if they have any opinions... Not sure what the right approach is.\n\nFor 2, I am not sure what the best approach is either. Even if we had an `InvalidStateError` type, there are multiple situations that would throw it or reject with it; it seems nice to be able to distinguish. Should I just not worry about it?\n",
